## HTML

1. Utilize semantic tags as much as possible.

- Using semantic HTML elements is crucial for accessibility purposes.

```html
Bad:
<div className={cx(classes.root, 'mt-modal')}>
  <div className='mt-modal-header'>
    <span className={cx(classes.header, 'modal-title')}>{t('modal.title')}</span>
  </div>
</div>
Good:
<section className={cx(classes.root, 'mt-modal')}>
  <header className='mt-modal-header'>
    <h3 className={cx(classes.header, 'modal-title')}>{t('modal.title')}</h3>
  </header>
</section>
```

3. Ensure that inline elements do not contain block elements

```html
Anti-pattern
<span>
  <p>Some text</p>
</span
```

4. Make sure that interactive elements (buttons, links, form elements etc.) have clear focus indication

5. Keep the DOM tree as small as possible. Avoid using redundant wrappers

- When adding extra wrappers to your code for styling and/or positioning purposes, consider using different Flexbox and Grid techniques to avoid extra DOM nodes

```html
Bad:
<div className={classes.root}>
  <div className={classes.wrapper}>
    <div className={classes.innerWrapper}>
      <!-- content -->
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
Good:
<div className={classes.root}>
  <!-- content -->
</div>
```
